## Summary
Adds an implementation-independent Python oracle for metrology traceability pyramids, primary-anchor classification, conservative reference-standard aging, and decision-qualified traceability.

### Core theorem
A measurement chain may be internally consistent yet still be unfit for a decision. A critical measurement needs a complete, non-circular path to a declared primary anchor, every element must still be valid, and the accumulated conservative error bound must fit the decision allowance.

### Semantics
- explicit `LocalPrimary`, `ImportedPrimary`, and `Derived` references;
- derived references require an explicit parent;
- primary anchors have no parent;
- missing parents and traceability cycles fail closed;
- per-reference bounded error ages conservatively with non-negative drift;
- path bounds sum across the entire metrology pyramid;
- exact decision-bound equality is accepted with a deterministic floating comparison epsilon;
- expiration of any path element invalidates the whole chain;
- local anchor + sufficient precision => `QualifiedLocalTraceability`;
- imported anchor + sufficient precision => `QualifiedImportTraceability`;
- a local chain can still be `InsufficientPrecision`;
- deterministic metadata-sensitive evidence digest.

### Independent execution evidence
The final candidate self-test was executed locally with Python 3 on 2026-09-13 and returned `ok`.

The fixture covers imported/local anchor qualification, locally anchored but insufficient precision, conservative aging, exact-bound acceptance, expiry, missing-root rejection, unsupported-cycle rejection, deterministic digesting, and malformed-input rejection.

### Boundaries
This is a deterministic structural/bounded-error traceability reference. It is not an SI realization, calibration-physics model, GUM covariance analysis, certification system, artifact-aging predictor, cyber-authenticity mechanism, or hardware qualification.

